WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.434 --> 00:00:02.702
-Our next guest is
a Tony Award-winning playwright

00:00:02.702 --> 00:00:05.138
and performer who you know
from her work on Broadway

00:00:05.138 --> 00:00:07.140
in "Sell/Buy/Date"
and "Bridge and Tunnel."

00:00:07.140 --> 00:00:10.343
Her new film, "Sell/Buy/Date,"
inspired by her Broadway show,

00:00:10.343 --> 00:00:11.778
is available now on demand.

00:00:11.778 --> 00:00:12.812
Let's take a look.

00:00:12.812 --> 00:00:14.847
-Okay. Look.
I know everybody's upset.

00:00:14.847 --> 00:00:15.981
I mean not just us.

00:00:15.981 --> 00:00:18.718
Everybody on both sides
of this debate.

00:00:18.718 --> 00:00:20.386
Is sex work empowering?

00:00:20.386 --> 00:00:21.520
Is it exploitation?

00:00:21.520 --> 00:00:22.756
There's no way to win.

00:00:22.756 --> 00:00:24.757
So, Sarah Jones,
we've pooled our money,

00:00:24.757 --> 00:00:26.893
which is really
the last of your money.

00:00:26.893 --> 00:00:28.261
And we're getting you
off the grid.

00:00:28.261 --> 00:00:31.097
-Wait, Narida, I really
appreciate this.

00:00:31.097 --> 00:00:33.566
I'm sorry I have
been such a mess.

00:00:33.566 --> 00:00:37.337
And now it seems like overnight,
everything's a [bleep]-nado

00:00:37.337 --> 00:00:38.904
even though
I still don't understand

00:00:38.904 --> 00:00:40.272
exactly what's going on.

00:00:40.272 --> 00:00:41.808
-Have you seen the tweets?

00:00:41.808 --> 00:00:43.777
What, do I need a bodyguard now?

00:00:43.777 --> 00:00:45.411
-Please welcome back to the show

00:00:45.411 --> 00:00:47.180
our friend Sarah Jones,
everybody.

00:00:47.180 --> 00:00:48.781
♪♪
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:48.781 --> 00:00:58.024 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:58.024 --> 00:01:07.266 line:0%
♪♪

00:01:07.266 --> 00:01:09.501
Welcome back.
I'm so happy to have you here.

00:01:09.501 --> 00:01:11.203
-I'm so happy to be here.

00:01:11.203 --> 00:01:14.273
-So you were here in 2016
for your Broadway show.

00:01:14.273 --> 00:01:18.478
This is the film adaptation of
it, but it's a very novel way

00:01:18.478 --> 00:01:23.482
to adapt it because it is a film
about the reaction to the fact

00:01:23.482 --> 00:01:26.085
that you were going to adapt
your play into a film.

00:01:26.085 --> 00:01:28.220
[ Laughter ]
-Did everybody follow?

00:01:28.220 --> 00:01:30.490
There's going to be a test.
-Okay, good.

00:01:30.490 --> 00:01:32.124
-So, yes.

00:01:32.124 --> 00:01:35.895
You know, we -- I did this play,
and you met some of the people.

00:01:35.895 --> 00:01:37.564
Just now you saw the clip.

00:01:37.564 --> 00:01:39.665
"I was here last time, Seth.

00:01:39.665 --> 00:01:40.800
I don't remember
if you remember me."

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:42.202
-I do. Now I do.
-Hi there.

00:01:42.202 --> 00:01:43.903
-You seem very familiar now.

00:01:43.903 --> 00:01:46.873
-Listen, because it was
the before times.

00:01:46.873 --> 00:01:49.042
I don't know how any of us are
still here.

00:01:49.042 --> 00:01:53.412
Anyway, so Lorraine and the
other characters from the play

00:01:53.412 --> 00:01:55.114
migrated into this movie.

00:01:55.114 --> 00:01:57.416
We had this announcement
that was very splashy.

00:01:57.416 --> 00:01:59.185
We were going to do a movie
based on the --

00:01:59.185 --> 00:02:00.953
you know, sort of inspired by
the play.

00:02:00.953 --> 00:02:03.189
And then all hell broke loose.

00:02:03.189 --> 00:02:08.127
Basically, there was a backlash
because the play talks about

00:02:08.127 --> 00:02:10.964
women and sex and power and,
you know, all the things that

00:02:10.964 --> 00:02:13.566
are relevant that we all just --
I mean, one of the characters

00:02:13.566 --> 00:02:16.969
in the movie actually, who was
in the play -- Hi, Seth.

00:02:16.969 --> 00:02:18.771
My name's Bella. Hi, everybody.

00:02:18.771 --> 00:02:21.441
I'm just, like, super stoked
to be here right now.

00:02:21.441 --> 00:02:23.543
And also, like, as a member of
Gen Z,

00:02:23.543 --> 00:02:25.211
just, like,
you're welcome for like --

00:02:25.211 --> 00:02:26.412
[ Laughter ]

00:02:26.412 --> 00:02:28.681
Saving, like,
all of you collectively."

00:02:28.681 --> 00:02:31.717
[ Laughter ]
Anyway, so Bella and all of us,

00:02:31.717 --> 00:02:33.319
as we start to do this movie,

00:02:33.319 --> 00:02:35.888
there was a backlash
on social media.

00:02:35.888 --> 00:02:37.623
"You're not allowed
to talk about this.

00:02:37.623 --> 00:02:39.525
We're going to cancel you.
We're going to shut you down."

00:02:39.525 --> 00:02:42.561
And I and all my characters
were like, "What, what now?"

00:02:42.561 --> 00:02:44.831
It was just kind of like --
But I'm trying to talk about

00:02:44.831 --> 00:02:48.434
an important topic, you know?
-And then it strikes me that

00:02:48.434 --> 00:02:51.637
maybe if there was an upside
to the backlash,

00:02:51.637 --> 00:02:54.974
it must have helped you take it
as seriously as you do

00:02:54.974 --> 00:02:57.343
when you went to make this film.
-Yes.

00:02:57.343 --> 00:03:00.746
I mean, first of all, the play
tried to, you know, look at

00:03:00.746 --> 00:03:02.915
different sides of a topic that,
let's face it --

00:03:02.915 --> 00:03:04.917
It's taboo,
but it's everywhere, right?

00:03:04.917 --> 00:03:06.352
People are like, "Oh, I don't
want to talk about that."

00:03:06.352 --> 00:03:08.554
Have you seen your kid's phone?

00:03:08.554 --> 00:03:11.191
I'm sorry, but everybody's
browsing history is like,

00:03:11.191 --> 00:03:12.458
"Just a second.
I'll be right back."

00:03:12.458 --> 00:03:13.592
[ Laughter ]
You know.

00:03:13.592 --> 00:03:15.995
And we're human beings
and sex is normal.

00:03:15.995 --> 00:03:18.764
You know, we need to be ready
to talk about all of this.

00:03:18.764 --> 00:03:21.100
And so in the play, I presented
a lot of different viewpoints.

00:03:21.100 --> 00:03:24.203
But when it was time to do the
movie, people were afraid that

00:03:24.203 --> 00:03:27.573
I was an outsider, maybe I'm not
allowed to talk about it.

00:03:27.573 --> 00:03:30.409
And I sort of felt like
they were like,

00:03:30.409 --> 00:03:32.745
"Uh, you're going to --
Give me that mike!"

00:03:32.745 --> 00:03:34.247
And I was like, "Wait, wait.
I'm trying

00:03:34.247 --> 00:03:36.281
to hold the mike
up to your mouth.

00:03:36.281 --> 00:03:37.583
Don't break my wrist.

00:03:37.583 --> 00:03:39.318
It's going to
ruin the whole thing.""

00:03:39.318 --> 00:03:44.957
So I think really my goal was
to do the topic justice, and I

00:03:44.957 --> 00:03:48.093
think we don't realize how close
this topic is to all of us.

00:03:48.093 --> 00:03:51.297
I mean, like, the Barbie doll
is actually based on

00:03:51.297 --> 00:03:53.132
a German prostitute
from World War II.

00:03:53.132 --> 00:03:54.634
I promise you
I did not know that.

00:03:54.634 --> 00:03:55.668
[ Laughter ]

00:03:55.668 --> 00:03:57.203
All through my Barbie
experiences,

00:03:57.203 --> 00:03:59.005
I wasn't kind of like,
"This is -- Hmm."

00:03:59.005 --> 00:04:01.207
You know, like -- Anyway, so I
think it's interesting.

00:04:01.207 --> 00:04:04.143
-And I feel like -- you know,
correct me if I'm wrong,

00:04:04.143 --> 00:04:05.945
but the way you build
your characters,

00:04:05.945 --> 00:04:08.147
the way you've always done it
is by talking to people,

00:04:08.147 --> 00:04:10.783
is by listening to people,
whether it's to, you know, learn

00:04:10.783 --> 00:04:13.052
the way they talk
or learn their experiences.

00:04:13.052 --> 00:04:16.255
And I know you talk to activists
and advocates and lawyers,

00:04:16.255 --> 00:04:18.424
and you build a breadth of
people that

00:04:18.424 --> 00:04:20.526
you can then bring
to the experience.

00:04:20.526 --> 00:04:23.829
So, yes, you're the only one
talking, but there are, because

00:04:23.829 --> 00:04:25.431
of the way you've always
performed,

00:04:25.431 --> 00:04:27.333
there are a litany of voices.

00:04:27.333 --> 00:04:29.935
-That's what I wanted people to
understand, is that I come from

00:04:29.935 --> 00:04:31.904
this multicultural family
in the first place

00:04:31.904 --> 00:04:33.506
where all my characters
come from.

00:04:33.506 --> 00:04:36.142
One who's in the movie,
Narida -- First of all,

00:04:36.142 --> 00:04:37.677
hi, Seth. I'm sorry I haven't
been here before,

00:04:37.677 --> 00:04:40.145
but I'm half Dominican,
half Puerto Rican, all proud.

00:04:40.145 --> 00:04:41.881
Sarah Jones didn't want me to
come out here because she knows

00:04:41.881 --> 00:04:43.349
when I'm nervous, I start to
talk really fast,

00:04:43.349 --> 00:04:44.483
and nobody can understand
anything I'm saying.

00:04:44.483 --> 00:04:45.884
But I'm sorry, I just had to
come in here

00:04:45.884 --> 00:04:47.353
because I wanted to represent.
Anyway, I'm going to stop,

00:04:47.353 --> 00:04:50.122
but I just wanted to say that
actually because the movie is

00:04:50.122 --> 00:04:52.392
about women and sex and power
and all of us,

00:04:52.392 --> 00:04:54.627
you know, like, this is the kind
of thing that hopefully people

00:04:54.627 --> 00:04:56.562
will -- I mean maybe it will
make your Thanksgiving

00:04:56.562 --> 00:04:58.297
conversation a little bit
more spicy.

00:04:58.297 --> 00:05:01.133
But we really have to be able to
talk about all of this stuff.

00:05:01.133 --> 00:05:03.435
Anyway, so, yes,
all of my characters,

00:05:03.435 --> 00:05:05.737
the goal was
to talk to activists,

00:05:05.737 --> 00:05:10.543
to talk to people who are
experienced, whether they are,

00:05:10.543 --> 00:05:13.212
you know, kind of advocates,
or lawyers, or people --

00:05:13.212 --> 00:05:16.715
Listen, I learned so much about
kind of the sex industry.

00:05:16.715 --> 00:05:19.518
We don't think of it this way,
but even a wholesome family who

00:05:19.518 --> 00:05:22.254
goes to Hooters and takes their
kids -- Yeah, you're having

00:05:22.254 --> 00:05:24.290
your wings, but there's a side
of sex industry

00:05:24.290 --> 00:05:25.858
with those wings, you know?

00:05:25.858 --> 00:05:28.927
So, for all of us,
I think on the journey,

00:05:28.927 --> 00:05:31.430
I got to learn from
other people.

00:05:31.430 --> 00:05:34.600
I didn't -- I don't want people
to come to this thinking

00:05:34.600 --> 00:05:37.269
it's only Julia Roberts
in "Pretty Woman."

00:05:37.269 --> 00:05:39.705
We love the thigh-high boots.

00:05:39.705 --> 00:05:41.607
But, you know, there's more
to it than that.

00:05:41.607 --> 00:05:44.209
And it's also not --
I don't want to stigmatize

00:05:44.209 --> 00:05:47.446
and slut-shame women for the
stuff that guys are like --

00:05:47.446 --> 00:05:49.048
"Hey!" You know,
it's like, alright,

00:05:49.048 --> 00:05:50.616
let's actually have
a bigger conversation.

00:05:50.616 --> 00:05:52.217
-Your mom was in the film.

00:05:52.217 --> 00:05:54.253
Was it tough to convince her
to do it?

00:05:54.253 --> 00:05:56.889
-Seth, speaking of Thanksgiving,
uh, uh,

00:05:56.889 --> 00:05:58.791
does anybody have an extra place
this year?

00:05:58.791 --> 00:06:00.326
[ Laughter ]
No, no.

00:06:00.326 --> 00:06:01.827
Actually, my mom's amazing.

00:06:01.827 --> 00:06:05.464
She's an OB/GYN herself, so she
cares about, you know, health

00:06:05.464 --> 00:06:06.732
and women's health
and all the stuff

00:06:06.732 --> 00:06:08.334
that's happening in the country.

00:06:08.334 --> 00:06:11.803
And so I remember just saying,
"Mom, be yourself."

00:06:11.803 --> 00:06:13.406
People think we scripted
the stuff.

00:06:13.406 --> 00:06:15.708
When you see this movie,
my mom --

00:06:15.708 --> 00:06:17.143
You'll understand why
I'm in therapy.

00:06:17.143 --> 00:06:19.945
[ Laughter ]
But she's also amazing,

00:06:19.945 --> 00:06:22.014
but there is one scene --
I won't give it away,

00:06:22.014 --> 00:06:24.116
but let's just say you never
want to turn and hear your mom

00:06:24.116 --> 00:06:25.852
say, "Is that person
wearing a strap-on?"

00:06:25.852 --> 00:06:27.286
[ Laughter ]

00:06:27.286 --> 00:06:30.656
[ Stammering ]
It was not standard --

00:06:30.656 --> 00:06:32.758
standard mother/daughter
conversation.

00:06:32.758 --> 00:06:35.761
-It's not about -- It's not like
a mother/daughter bonding

00:06:35.761 --> 00:06:38.531
journey a lot of people take.
-It's a different one.

00:06:38.531 --> 00:06:42.267
It's a little different.
But I do believe people are,

00:06:42.267 --> 00:06:47.239
you know, ready to have kind of
more open, you know,

00:06:47.239 --> 00:06:49.108
relationship to
all of this stuff.

00:06:49.108 --> 00:06:51.811
You know, we're all so
polarized, and it can be so easy

00:06:51.811 --> 00:06:55.714
to kind of feel like we just
shouldn't talk about anything.

00:06:55.714 --> 00:06:57.016
But that just makes it worse.

00:06:57.016 --> 00:06:58.851
So hopefully this movie is a way

00:06:58.851 --> 00:07:01.220
for people to enter
into the conversation.

00:07:01.220 --> 00:07:03.856
We joke that it's
a wonderful holiday gift.

00:07:03.856 --> 00:07:04.857
The holidays are coming.
-Yes.

00:07:04.857 --> 00:07:05.958
-Cheaper than socks

00:07:05.958 --> 00:07:07.826
and hopefully more substantive.

00:07:07.826 --> 00:07:10.029
-There you go. I think that's
a great argument for that.

00:07:10.029 --> 00:07:12.331
And thank you so much for being
here and for making the film

00:07:12.331 --> 00:07:14.200
and for doing everything you do
onstage as well.

00:07:14.200 --> 00:07:15.401
Sarah Jones.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:07:15.401 --> 00:07:17.703
"Sell/Buy/Date" available
on demand

00:07:17.703 --> 00:07:19.705
on Amazon Prime Video,
Apple TV.

00:07:19.705 --> 00:07:21.374
We'll be right back with
more "Late Night."

